  "enhanced_information": "The clinical presentation of a previously healthy 4-year-old boy with fever, flaccid blisters that rupture easily, and a positive Nikolsky sign is highly suggestive of Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome (SSSS). The presence of a high leukocyte count and elevated ESR indicates an inflammatory process, while the normal oropharyngeal examination helps exclude infectious causes like scarlet fever. Pemphigus vulgaris is less likely due to the patient's age and the acute presentation, as it typically occurs in older individuals. Toxic epidermal necrolysis and bullous pemphigoid are also less likely given the age and clinical features. Therefore, the most consistent diagnosis is Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome, supported by the evidence of a possible MRSA infection.",
